course: biology
lesson: 200
title: Rabbits: hay not carrots, binkies and teeth that never stop growing
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Bugs Bunny munches a carrot in every cartoon, so many people think carrots are what rabbits should eat. Real rabbits are built for grass and hay, and carrots are only a small sweet treat.
explain: A rabbit's front teeth never stop growing, a few millimetres every week, and chewing tough grass and hay all day is what wears them down to the right length.
explain: A rabbit is a prey animal: its eyes are on the sides of its head, its long ears swivel to catch danger, and when it is scared it thumps its back foot to warn others.
explain: When a rabbit feels safe and happy it may leap into the air and twist, a «binky», or flop over onto its side, which looks alarming but means it is relaxed.
fact: Wild rabbits almost never find carrots; they eat grass, clover and other leafy plants, and a pet rabbit's diet should be mostly hay and grass with some leafy greens.
fact: Carrots are sweet, so too many can make a rabbit fat and upset its tummy; carrot tops, the green leaves, are healthier for a rabbit than the orange root.
fact: Loving a food does not make it healthy: a rabbit fed mostly carrots and pellets can get bad teeth, tummy trouble and weight problems, just as a child would on sweets.
fact: A rabbit's teeth grow all its life; without enough hay to chew they can grow into sharp spikes that cut the mouth, so vets check rabbits' teeth.
fact: A rabbit's gut needs hay moving through it all the time, and a rabbit that stops eating or pooing for half a day needs a vet urgently.
fact: Rabbits eat some of their own droppings, soft ones made at night, to digest their food a second time; this is normal and healthy, and the hard round droppings you see are the ones they do not eat.
fact: A happy rabbit may do a binky, leaping and twisting in the air, or flop suddenly onto its side to rest.
fact: A rabbit thumps its back foot on the ground to warn of danger, because rabbits are prey animals and always on the lookout.
fact: A rabbit must never be lifted by its ears; hold it with one hand under its chest and one under its bottom, close to your body, because it can kick and hurt its back.
fact: Rabbits are social animals that need lots of space to run and a rabbit friend; pet rabbits can live eight to twelve years.
fact: Each long ear can turn on its own to catch sounds from different directions, and the ears also help release heat on hot days.
fact: Wild rabbits live in groups in a network of burrows called a warren; hares are different, living above ground, with babies born furry and with open eyes, while baby rabbits are born bald and blind in the burrow.
exercise: What should a pet rabbit eat most of? | Hay and grass | Carrots every day | Lettuce and nothing else | Bread and pellets
exercise: What do wild rabbits really eat? | Grass, clover and leafy plants | Mostly carrots pulled up from farmers' fields | Seeds and nuts only | Insects and worms
exercise: A girl gives her rabbit three big carrots a day. What can happen? | The rabbit gets fat and its tummy gets upset | The rabbit can see in the dark, just as grown-ups always say | The rabbit becomes very healthy | The rabbit's ears grow longer
exercise: Which part of the carrot is healthier for a rabbit? | The green leafy tops | The crunchy orange root | The little seeds inside | Carrot juice in a bowl
exercise: A boy argues that rabbits LOVE carrots, so they must be good for them. What is right? | Loving a food does not make it healthy | Anything a rabbit loves is healthy | Rabbits only love healthy food | Rabbits hate carrots in real life
exercise: Why does a vet check a rabbit's front teeth? | They grow all the time and can grow too long | They fall out every year and new ones must grow in | Rabbits have no back teeth | Rabbit teeth turn blue when ill, so the vet looks at the colour
exercise: What keeps a rabbit's teeth at the right length? | Chewing hay and grass all day | Brushing them gently every night | Biting the metal bars of the cage | Crunching hard carrots every day
exercise: Why does a rabbit chew hay almost all day? | Its gut and teeth need it | It is bored and has nothing else to do | It is building a nest for the winter | It is looking for carrots hidden in it
exercise: A rabbit has not eaten or pooed for half a day. What should happen? | Take it to a vet urgently | Wait a week and see | Give it a carrot | Put it in a smaller hutch
exercise: A rabbit eats some of its own droppings. Is this normal? | Yes, it digests its food a second time | No, it means it is very ill and must see a vet today | No, it is starving | Yes, but only when it is a baby
exercise: A rabbit jumps and twists in the air. What is this called? | A binky | A thump | A hop-scotch | A flip-flop
exercise: Why does a rabbit thump its back foot on the floor? | To warn of danger | To dig a hole in the floor | Because its foot is itchy | To ask you for more food
exercise: How should you lift a rabbit? | One hand under its chest, one under its bottom, held close | By its ears, like a magician pulling it out of a hat on stage | By its back legs | By the scruff of the neck
exercise: Is a small hutch alone enough for a rabbit? | No, it needs lots of space and a rabbit friend | Yes, rabbits like to be alone in a small dark box | Yes, if it gets carrots | No, it needs a cat friend to keep it company
exercise: How long can pet rabbits live? | About eight to twelve years | About one year | About three months | About forty years
exercise: Why do a rabbit's ears turn in different directions? | Each ear moves on its own to catch sounds | They are trying to fly, like the bunny in the cartoon | They point to food | They follow the sun
exercise: A wild rabbit runs into a hole in a field. What is its home called? | A warren | A drey | A sett | A nest box
exercise: How are hares different from rabbits? | Hares live above ground and their babies are born furry with open eyes | Hares live in warrens deep underground and their babies are born bald and blind | Hares are baby rabbits | Hares have no ears
test: «Bugs Bunny eats carrots all day and he's fine, so I'll do the same for Thumper» What is right? | Real rabbits need mostly hay; carrots are just a small treat | Yes, carrots are a full rabbit diet, as long as he gets plenty of them | Bugs Bunny is a real rabbit | Rabbits must never see a carrot
test: «he goes MAD for carrots so they must be good for him, duh» What is right? | Liking a food does not make it healthy, like sweets for us | Yes, animals only like healthy food | Yes, the more the better | No, rabbits hate carrots
test: «ew the rabbit's eating its own poo, it's sick!» What is true? | It is normal; it eats special soft droppings to digest food again | It is starving | It is very ill and dying, so it must go to the vet straight away tonight | It is copying the dog
test: What would happen if a rabbit ate only soft food and no hay for months? | Its teeth could grow too long and cut its mouth | Its teeth would fall out | Its ears would shrink | Nothing at all would change, because rabbits are fine on soft food only
test: «Flopsy just flipped onto her side, is she dead??» but she is breathing and calm. What is likely? | She is relaxed, a flop means a happy rabbit | She has fainted from fear | She is ill and dying | She is trying to dig
test: «she's leaping and twisting round the garden like a lunatic» What does it mean? | It is a binky, she is happy | She has fleas | She is angry with you | She is scared of the grass
test: «pick him up by the ears like the magician did» What is right? | Never, lift him with one hand under the chest and one under the bottom | Yes, ears are handles, and that is why rabbits have such long ones | Yes, but only for small rabbits | No, pick him up by the tail
test: «the bunny keeps thumping its foot, is it doing a dance» What is it doing? | Warning of danger | Dancing to the music on the radio | Asking for carrots | Practising for running
test: Count them: two rabbits sit in a run. How many long ears are listening? | 4 | 2 | 8 | 6
test: Count them: a rabbit, a hare and Bugs Bunny line up. How many tails? | 3 | 2 | 6 | 0
test: «keep him in the little hutch on his own, saves money» What is right? | Rabbits need much more space and a rabbit friend | Rabbits like to be alone in small hutches where nobody can bother them | It is fine if he gets carrots | Rabbits sleep all the time anyway
test: In a cartoon a baby hare is born bald and blind in a burrow. What is true? | That fits baby rabbits; baby hares are born furry with open eyes above ground | Baby hares are born bald and blind in burrows, just like the cartoon shows | Hares and rabbits are the same animal | Baby rabbits and hares are both born furry
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the appropriate diet of the domestic rabbit? | Hay and grass ought to constitute the greater part, with carrots offered sparingly | Carrots ought to constitute the greater part of the diet | Lettuce ought to constitute the exclusive diet | Bread and pellets suffice entirely
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the teeth of a rabbit require regular inspection. | They grow continuously and may become overgrown without sufficient chewing of hay | They are shed annually | They are coloured differently each season, the colour indicating the health of the animal | Rabbits possess no teeth at the rear of the mouth
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the consumption by a rabbit of a portion of its own droppings. | It is a normal process permitting the further digestion of food | It invariably indicates grave illness | It indicates that the animal is starving | It is observed solely in captive animals
exam: It is given that a child proposes to lift a rabbit by its ears. Ascertain the correct method of handling. | One hand beneath the chest and one beneath the hindquarters, the animal held close | Suspension by the ears, as practised by magicians, the ears being sufficiently strong | Suspension by the hind legs | Suspension by the tail
